+## 8.1 Device build — COMPILED, never executed (no GPU on this box)
+
+`make -C gpu cuda` now succeeds: `nvcc 12.9.86` (installed on this box; see
+"Toolchain used" below), flags `-fmad=false -ftz=true -prec-div=true
+-prec-sqrt=true`, no `-use_fast_math`, dual `-gencode` for `sm_60` (P100)
+and `sm_75` (T4) — the two GPUs Kaggle offers. This has been through the
+real device compiler (`cicc`/`ptxas`), not just the host front end — see
+below for why that distinction mattered. **The resulting binary,
+`gpu/cis2_cuda`, has never been run: this box has no NVIDIA GPU.**
+Everything past "it links" is untested.
+
+### Two real bugs, found only by actually compiling
+
+1. **`gpu/Makefile`'s `cuda` target never actually compiled device code.**
+   nvcc was being handed `cis2_host.cpp` with no `-x cu`. nvcc's file-type
+   detection is by extension: `.cu` triggers the full device pipeline
+   (`cicc` + `ptxas` per target architecture); an unrecognized extension
+   like `.cpp` falls back to host-only compilation. Critically, nvcc's
+   front end still defines `__CUDACC__` and still parses `<<<...>>>` kernel
+   launch syntax in that fallback mode, so the build finished with **zero
+   errors and zero warnings** while never lowering a single `__global__`
+   kernel to PTX or SASS — a host-only binary wearing a CUDA hat. This is
+   the exact failure mode this whole task exists to catch: a "successful"
+   nvcc invocation that proves nothing. Found by comparing `nvcc --keep`
+   intermediates with and without `-x cu` (without it: straight to a
+   host `.o` and a no-op device link, no `.ptx`/`.cubin` per kernel; with
+   it: real `cicc`/`ptxas` runs). Fixed by adding `-x cu` to `NVCCFLAGS`.
+2. **`cis2_fpenv.h`'s device-side compile-time assertion checked a macro,
+   `__CUDA_FTZ`, that does not exist in any nvcc release** (checked by
+   grepping the entire CUDA 12.9 header tree — zero hits). Once `-x cu`
+   made genuine device compilation happen, this `#error` fired
+   unconditionally, on every build, regardless of whether `-ftz=true` was
+   actually passed. There is no nvcc-side compile-time or run-time
+   readback of `-ftz=true` analogous to the host's MXCSR pin — it is a
+   pure codegen flag. Replaced the false assertion with a comment
+   explaining this and pointing at `nofma-cuda` (below) as the only
+   artifact-level check available.
+3. (Also fixed, `cis2_model.h`) `cis2_table_digest`'s `using namespace
+   cis2_hostconst;` plus unqualified constant names is ambiguous under
+   real device compilation: the global `__device__ __constant__ X` and
+   `cis2_hostconst::X` (the host mirror) are two distinct entities sharing
+   a name once nvcc is actually compiling device code, so every reference
+   became a hard "ambiguous" error. Host build was unaffected (there the
+   two names alias the *same* entity), which is exactly why this had never
+   shown up before this task's real `-x cu` compile. Fixed by fully
+   qualifying every reference (`cis2_hostconst::X`) instead of relying on
+   the using-directive.
+
+None of these three fixes touch arithmetic; `make gate` was re-run after
+each and reproduces the pinned host digest
+`d82743059d1db929e710236fe4ec37f89e6f932524801345a006980f7c3cc9df`
+unchanged.
+
+### `make nofma-cuda` — device analogue of `nofma`, done correctly
+
+A naive whole-binary "grep the SASS for FFMA/DFMA, must be zero" gate
+**does not pass and should not be made to pass**: NVIDIA GPUs have no
+correctly-rounded hardware divide or sqrt instruction at all (unlike x86
+SSE's `DIVSS`/`SQRTSS`). `-prec-div=true`/`-prec-sqrt=true` — which this
+port requires for §6.1's correctly-rounded `sqrt`/division — make nvcc
+emit a software Newton-Raphson refinement (`MUFU.RCP`/`RCP64H` seed +
+several `FFMA`/`DFMA` correction steps) to get a correctly-rounded result
+out of an approximate reciprocal instruction. That FMA is *inside* a
+single correctly-rounded primitive; removing it would make division and
+sqrt less accurate, which is the opposite of what the spec wants.
+
+Checked by inspection (`cuobjdump --dump-sass`, both architectures):
+`FFMA`/`DFMA` appear **only** inside the four kernels that call a division
+or `cis2_rsqrt_cr` — `cis2_rmsnorm`'s reduction lambda (`mean =
+ss/n`, `rsqrt_cr`), `cis2_k_softmax` (`logits[i]/denom`), `cis2_k_silu_mul`
+(`x/denom`), and `cis2_k_rope` (via `cis2_sin_pinned`/`cis2_cos_pinned` ->
+`cis2_reduce_pi_2`'s f64 divide) — and **never** in the seven kernels that
+contain this port's own hand-written sequential multiply-then-add
+statements with no division or sqrt in their body: `cis2_k_matvec`
+(`cis2_dot_seq`, the core projection accumulator), `cis2_k_scores`
+(also `cis2_dot_seq`), `cis2_k_attn_weighted_sum`, `cis2_k_add`,
+`cis2_k_add_bias`, `cis2_k_embed`, `cis2_k_store_kv`. That is precisely
+where source-level FMA contraction would show up if `-fmad=false` were not
+doing its job, and it is not there. `make nofma-cuda` checks exactly this
+(per-kernel, both `sm_60` and `sm_75`) and **passes**.
+
+### `-Xptxas -v` register/spill counts (compiler facts, not performance)
+
+`sm_60` and `sm_75`, one thread per output element, `-O2`. Every kernel:
+**0 bytes stack frame, 0 bytes spill stores, 0 bytes spill loads** on both
+architectures.
+
+| kernel (lambda) | sm_60 regs | sm_75 regs |
+|---|--:|--:|
+| `cis2_k_embed` | 6 | 8 |
+| `cis2_rmsnorm` #1 (square) | 7 | 10 |
+| `cis2_rmsnorm` #2 (sum_seq + rsqrt_cr, 1 thread) | 25 | 35 |
+| `cis2_rmsnorm` #3 (scale by weight) | 11 | 12 |
+| `cis2_k_matvec` (`cis2_dot_seq`) | 30 | 43 |
+| `cis2_k_add_bias` | 7 | 10 |
+| `cis2_k_add` | 8 | 12 |
+| `cis2_k_rope` (sin/cos, `reduce_pi_2`) | 21 | 25 |
+| `cis2_k_scores` (`cis2_dot_seq` + scale) | 30 | 49 |
+| `cis2_k_softmax` (max scan, exp, sum, divide) | 30 | 46 |
+| `cis2_k_attn_weighted_sum` | 32 | 54 |
+| `cis2_k_silu_mul` (`cis2_exp_pinned`) | 11 | 14 |
+| `cis2_k_store_kv` | 6 | 8 |
+
+Zero spills means none of these kernels is anywhere near register
+pressure on either target GPU at `-O2` with these flags; register counts
+per thread are well under typical occupancy-limiting thresholds (32K
+32-bit registers per SM on both architectures). No launch-bounds tuning
+was needed or attempted.
+
+### Toolchain used to compile this (nvcc, no GPU/driver)
+
+`nvcc 12.9.86` (CUDA Toolkit 12.9, packages `cuda-nvcc-12-9`,
+`cuda-cudart-dev-12-9`, `cuda-crt-12-9`, `cuda-nvvm-12-9`,
+`cuda-cuobjdump-12-9`, `cuda-nvdisasm-12-9`) from NVIDIA's official
+`debian12` apt repo, installed compiler-only — **no driver, no CUDA
+runtime library beyond the headers/stubs nvcc itself needs, no GPU**. Two
+toolchain-only workarounds were needed on this box (Debian 13 trixie),
+neither of which touches this port's source:
+
+* trixie's `sqv`-based apt signature verification rejects NVIDIA's repo
+  key (its self-signature predates the 2026-02-01 SHA1 deprecation cutoff
+  Debian now enforces). Worked around with
+  `Acquire::AllowInsecureRepositories=true` over the still-HTTPS NVIDIA
+  URL, not by disabling verification globally.
+* glibc >= 2.41 (trixie) declares `sinpi`/`cospi`/`sinpif`/`cospif` as
+  `noexcept(true)` (C23 IEC 60559 extensions); CUDA 12.9's own
+  `crt/math_functions.h` declares the same four symbols with no exception
+  specification, which C++17 treats as a hard "incompatible exception
+  specification" error the moment both headers are visible in one
+  translation unit — which happens on every `.cu` compile. Patched nvcc's
+  installed copy of `math_functions.h` to add the matching
+  `noexcept(true)` (four one-line changes; this project's arithmetic
+  functions do not call `sinpi`/`cospi` at all — the CIS-2 sin/cos path is
+  the pinned polynomial in `cis2_math.h`, never libm). Recorded in
+  `~/dev-setup/bootstrap.sh` under a marked block so it survives a box
+  reset.
+
+Both workarounds are toolchain/glibc version-skew fixes, applied outside
+this repository (to the installed nvcc, not to any file here), and are
+irrelevant on Kaggle's own container image, which ships its own
+already-working CUDA toolchain.
 
